---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PageHero from '../PageHero.astro';

// English-only, and deliberately not part of the `partners` collection --
// see content.config.ts's comment on why per-partner pages were dropped
// twice before. This one is additive: Ananas is the one partner with a
// real multi-year project archive (design decks, land-use maps, planting
// plans) to draw on, not a return to a per-partner listing pattern.

const articleSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Article',
  headline: 'Ananas at TVC',
  description: "How Ananas, TVC's permaculture design partner since 2017, has shaped the farm's land and water systems.",
  image: new URL('/images/partners/ananas/syntropic-design-2020.jpg', Astro.site).toString(),
  author: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'Ananas', url: 'https://ananas.design/services' },
  publisher: {
    '@type': 'Organization',
    name: 'Tamarind Valley Collective',
    logo: { '@type': 'ImageObject', url: new URL('/images/brand/tvc-logo-mark.png', Astro.site).toString() },
  },
};
---

<BaseLayout
  lang="en"
  title="Ananas"
  description="How Ananas, TVC's permaculture design partner since 2017, has shaped the farm's land and water systems — from the Final Design Document and GAIA map to zone-by-zone earthworks and planting design."
  image="/images/partners/ananas/syntropic-design-2020.jpg"
  imageWidth={1600}
  imageHeight={1131}
  schema={[articleSchema]}
  translated={false}
>
  <PageHero
    eyebrow="Partners"
    title="Designing TVC's Land"
    intro="A closer look at what Ananas has actually designed here, drawn from the project archive built up since 2017."
  />

  <article class="section wrapper prose">
    <p>
      Ananas is a Bangalore-based regenerative design studio and TVC's permaculture design
      partner since 2017. The <a href="/people/partners">Partners page</a> covers who they are
      in a couple of lines — this page is a closer look at what they've actually designed here,
      drawn from the project archive of design decks, land-use maps, and planting plans built up
      over several seasons of work.
    </p>

    <h2>2017: Earthworks and a nursery</h2>
    <p>
      The earliest recorded designs date to December 2017: an earthworks, irrigation, and
      support-planting plan, a nursery sized to raise around 2,000 saplings a quarter, and a
      multi-layered living fence doing several jobs at once — drought-tolerant native species,
      water-catching "boomerangs," and a guild supporting everything planted around it.
    </p>

    <h2>2017–2018: The Farming Collective</h2>
    <p>
      Alongside the farm's own design, Ananas proposed "The Farming Collective," a broader
      community-farming concept. The groundwork behind it is its own small archive: check-dam
      and gully-plug studies, a riparian buffer-zone map protecting a stretch either side of the
      farm's nullah while densely planting the zone beyond it, and planting guidelines for the
      tree species suited to the site.
    </p>

    <h2>2019: An environmental study, then a full design document</h2>
    <p>
      2019 opened with a formal environmental study, which fed directly into April 2019's Final
      Design Document — the most detailed single record in the archive. It broke the farm into
      zones — Upper Arkavathy, Lower Arkavathy, and Kaveri, plus a dedicated forest-regeneration
      test site — each with its own pre-monsoon task list, and set the engagement's headline
      number: cutting water use from a baseline of 6.05 million litres a year by three-quarters
      by 2020. Upper Arkavathy's own gardens were designed around alternating raised beds for
      annuals and sunken beds for perennials, so the overflow from watering one recharges the
      other.
    </p>

    <div class="plan-pair">
      <figure class="poster">
        <img
          src="/images/partners/ananas/upper-arkavathy-plan.jpg"
          alt="Upper Arkavathy zone plan from the Final Design Document, showing plots, ponds, and planting areas in color"
          width="543"
          height="598"
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <figcaption>Upper Arkavathy zone plan</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure class="poster">
        <img
          src="/images/partners/ananas/kaveri-plan.jpg"
          alt="Kaveri zone plan from the Final Design Document, showing numbered plots, ponds, and rocky areas in color"
          width="739"
          height="628"
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <figcaption>Kaveri zone plan</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>

    <figure class="poster">
      <img
        src="/images/partners/ananas/bed-section-diagram.jpg"
        alt="Diagram of alternating raised beds for annuals and sunken beds for perennials, with interior trees and a pathway between them"
        width="1024"
        height="768"
        loading="lazy"
      />
      <figcaption>Raised beds for annuals, sunken beds for perennials — the section Ananas proposed for Upper Arkavathy's gardens</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <h2>June 2019: The GAIA map</h2>
    <p>
      The GAIA map is the farm's master drawing — swales, sheet-flow spreaders, "boomerangs," a
      traveling nursery, a wildlife buffer zone, causeways, forest-garden layers, gully
      plugs/check dams, and hard fencing, each annotated directly on the site plan alongside the
      zones it names (Upper and Lower Arkavathy, Upper and Lower Kaveri, the Temple Plot, and the
      "Island to Nowhere").
    </p>

    <figure class="gaia-figure">
      <a href="/images/partners/ananas/gaia-map.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
        <img
          src="/images/partners/ananas/gaia-map.jpg"
          alt="The GAIA land-use map, a fully annotated illustrated site plan of TVC showing zones, earthworks, planting systems, and design notes"
          width="1800"
          height="2563"
          loading="lazy"
        />
      </a>
      <figcaption>The GAIA land-use map — <a href="/images/partners/ananas/gaia-map.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">view full size ↗</a></figcaption>
    </figure>

    <h2>2020: A syntropic planting design</h2>
    <p>
      By April 2020 the design had moved into detailed bed-level planning: a syntropic
      agroforestry layout alternating tree rows — high, medium, and low canopy — with biomass
      rows and annual beds, fed by stone-lined channels and PVC piping off a shared water tank. A
      system meant to build soil and yield food in the same footprint.
    </p>

    <figure class="poster poster--wide">
      <img
        src="/images/partners/ananas/syntropic-design-2020.jpg"
        alt="TVC Syntropic bed design from April 2020, showing alternating tree rows, biomass rows, and annual beds with irrigation channels"
        width="1600"
        height="1131"
        loading="lazy"
      />
      <figcaption>TVC Syntropic bed design, April 2020</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <h2>What's still on the ground</h2>
    <p>
      The throughline across all of it is water: earthworks that slow, spread, and sink rainfall
      before growing anything on top of that base — food forest and living-fence planting, the
      nursery that keeps propagating it, and the community process that turned drawings into a
      farm actually run by resident families.
    </p>

    <p>
      Read the short version on <a href="/people/partners">our Partners page</a>, or visit <a href="https://ananas.design/services" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ananas.design</a>.
    </p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .prose :global(img) {
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    margin: 16px 0;
  }

  .prose :global(h2) {
    margin-top: 32px;
  }

  .plan-pair {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr);
    gap: 16px;
    max-width: 72ch;
    margin: 16px 0 32px;
  }

  .plan-pair .poster {
    width: 100%;
    max-width: none;
    margin: 0;
  }

  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .plan-pair {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }
  }

  .prose :global(.poster) {
    width: 100%;
    max-width: 360px;
    margin: 16px auto 32px;
  }

  /* The GAIA map is dense enough (small annotation text) that squeezing it
     into the same 360px poster column as the other figures would make it
     useless - let it run the full wrapper width instead, well past the
     72ch text column, same as the plan pair breaks out to that column's
     edge rather than staying inside prose's narrower default. It's
     portrait (rotated from its original landscape scan), so full wrapper
     width would just make it needlessly tall without adding legibility -
     capped and centered instead, same idea as .poster--wide. */
  .prose :global(.gaia-figure) {
    width: 100%;
    max-width: 720px;
    margin: 16px auto 32px;
  }

  .prose :global(.gaia-figure img) {
    margin: 0;
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    box-shadow: var(--shadow-soft);
    border: 1px solid var(--tvc-line);
  }

  .prose :global(.gaia-figure figcaption) {
    margin-top: 8px;
    text-align: center;
    font-size: 0.82rem;
    color: var(--tvc-ink-soft);
  }

  .prose :global(.poster--wide) {
    max-width: 620px;
  }

  .prose :global(.poster img) {
    margin: 0;
    box-shadow: var(--shadow-soft);
    border: 1px solid var(--tvc-line);
  }

  .prose :global(.poster figcaption) {
    margin-top: 8px;
    text-align: center;
    font-size: 0.82rem;
    color: var(--tvc-ink-soft);
  }
</style>
